Third-party component: goose (downloaded at the user's request)
--------------------------------------------------------------

duthub does not bundle, embed or redistribute goose. The goose agent CLI from
the AAIF/goose project (formerly block/goose) is installed on demand through the
in-app "tool downloads" page, using duthub's own downloader (duthub-tooldown),
and runs as a separate process. No goose bytes are compiled into `duthub.exe`
or shipped next to it.

  Source:    https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose
  Release:   https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/releases
  Artifact:  goose-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip (rolling "latest" release asset)
  License:   Apache License, Version 2.0
  Copyright: Copyright the AAIF/goose contributors

The downloaded program is not modified by duthub. The full Apache-2.0 license
text and any upstream NOTICE ship with the upstream release package, which is
the copy installed on this machine (and are also available from the source URL
above).

duthub does not pin a checksum for the rolling asset. The installer records the
measured SHA-256 of the downloaded archive in
`<config dir>/tools/goose/manifest.json` and marks it `checksumVerified: false`
(unverified download, stated honestly); the runtime re-hashes the installed
executable against that record before launch. Version gate: >= 1.60.2.

Each release ships a machine-readable provenance statement as
`licenses/goose/PROVENANCE.md` (source: `apps/desktop/assets/goose-distribution.md`).
See `docs/decisions/ADR-3-GOOSE-BUNDLE.md` §9 for the distribution contract and
`scripts/release-gate.ps1` for the release checks.

Third-party Rust crates inside duthub itself are covered by the CycloneDX SBOM
and cargo-audit report shipped under `sbom/`. Third-party crates inside goose
are the upstream release's responsibility; duthub does not audit them at release
time and says so explicitly (`sbom/goose-audit-status.txt` is marked
UNVERIFIED).

